# Line-ending policy.
#
# This repo is edited on Windows (core.autocrlf=true) but every script executes
# on Linux — inside the Apptainer container, on TACC Lonestar6, or under WSL.
# A CRLF working-tree copy of a shell script is FATAL there, not cosmetic:
#   * `set -euo pipefail\r`      -> "set: pipefail\r: invalid option name", exit 2
#   * a CRLF line in primers.txt -> the reverse primer carries a trailing \r and
#                                   cutadapt rejects it as a non-IUPAC adapter
#
# Forcing eol=lf here is the durable fix. Running dos2unix instead only helps
# until the next checkout, which re-applies autocrlf and silently regresses.
#
# After editing this file run:  git add --renormalize .
# Then verify it took:          git ls-files --eol | grep w/crlf   (expect no output)

* text=auto

# Executed on Linux — must be LF in the working tree, always.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf
*.pl    text eol=lf
*.R     text eol=lf
*.r     text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.def   text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Parsed by the pipeline as `name:fwd:rev`; a trailing \r corrupts the reverse
# primer. This file was committed with CRLF in the index — hence the explicit rule.
misc/primers.txt text eol=lf

# Biological data and binaries: never touch these.
*.fastq  -text
*.fastq.gz binary
*.fasta  -text
*.fa     -text
*.sif    binary
*.tar.gz binary
*.zip    binary
*.rds    binary
*.pdf    binary
